mips: Ingenic XBurst2 MXU3 (512-bit SIMD) ASE for T40/T41

The T40/T41 XBurst2 cores carry Ingenic's MXU3, a 512-bit SIMD coprocessor
(coprocessor 2).  Their 4.4.94 kernels enable it lazily: the first MXU3
instruction raises Coprocessor Unusable with Cause.CE = 2 (Config1.C2 is
set, Status.CU2 is not), the do_cpu handler sets Status.CU2 and retries,
and switch_to saves/restores the 512-bit register file with MXU3
load/store instructions.  Stock QEMU does not decode MXU3, so that vendor
userspace (libimp/libvenus/...) and the kernel's save/restore take an
illegal-instruction trap.

This adds the ASE: 32 x 512-bit vector registers (vpr0..31), 4 x 512-bit
accumulators (vsr0..3), 32 scalar VWR registers and the MXU control/status
register, in CPU state (reset-cleared, migrated as a vmstate subsection).
A new self-contained decoder (target/mips/tcg/mxu3_translate.c) and helper
(mxu3_helper.c), driven by a table generated from the Ingenic GCC 7.2
libopcodes opcode tables (mxu3-gen.c.inc), implement the instructions;
MXU3 instructions raise Coprocessor Unusable (CE=2) when Status.CU2 == 0.

Encodings, operand layouts and semantics were recovered only from the
Ingenic GCC 7.2 toolchain (its objdump/as opcode tables and the RTL its
__builtin_mxu3_* intrinsics expand to) and the kernel's mxuv3.c save/
restore sequences; the gather/shuffle (gt*/gshuf*), the sr*/s* reductions
and the NN-accelerator ops have opaque UNSPEC RTL and are implemented
best-effort or as register-preserving no-ops (flagged in mxu3_helper.c) so
code using them does not trap, but their results are not guaranteed.

Decode is gated by MIPS_HFLAG_CU2 (bit 30), the Status.CU2 hflag that the
MXU2 patch (0018) already recomputes in compute_hflags and includes in
MIPS_HFLAG_TMASK; this patch only extends that recompute to cover ASE_MXU3
(so the CU2-enable retry re-translates instead of reusing a stale TB) and
reuses 0018's vmstate-subsection and decode-hook plumbing.

Approximated mnemonics are reported, never silent: the first execution of
each one logs a LOG_UNIMP message (-d unimp) naming it, and
QEMU_MXU3_STATS=1 prints per-mnemonic execution counts of approximated
instructions at exit (QEMU_MXU3_STATS=all counts every MXU3 mnemonic).
Measured with QEMU_MXU3_STATS=all on the T40 ISP/sensor model with
libimp's IVS move detection running on its frames (the only MXU3 code in
the T40 libimp; majestic's motion detection uses the same
IMP_IVS_CreateMoveInterface): the only approximated mnemonics executed
were gtb and gt2w, and both are now exact:
 - gt2w/gt4w/gt8w/gt2d/gt4d/gt2q: the GCC RTL is plain vec_merge
   (vec_duplicate (vec_select rt [j, j+N, ...])) rd mask, i.e.
   rd.x[i*m+k] = rt.x[N*k+j], other lanes kept.
 - gtb: UNSPEC RTL.  Per 64-bit lane, even bytes to the low word, odd to
   the high word: the only lane split under which libimp's narrowing idiom
   "gtb x,x; gt2w x[0],x,0" maps 32 halfwords to 32 packed bytes without
   losing pixels.  Checked functionally: with it, IVS reports motion in
   exactly the quadrant of a synthetic moving block and catches a 16x16
   block near the detection threshold, which the alternatives (no-op,
   whole-register or 128-bit-lane split) miss; the odd half is the natural
   complement (no code reads it).
After that, no approximated mnemonic executes in that workload; the T41
SDK image's only application contains no MXU3 code.  Applies on
0001..0018.

The XBurst2 CPU model is updated in
0015-mips-ingenic-xburst-cpus.patch: Config1.C2 = 1, Status.CU2 made writable, and
ASE_MXU3 | ASE_MXU3_1 added to insn_flags.

